Recursion Quiz Practice Problems
}
\end{center}

\begin{note}
We call a function \emph{recursive} if it calls itself.
Every correct recursive function satisfies two laws:
\begin{enumerate}
\item in each recursive call, the input parameter somehow gets ``smaller'', and
\item there is a \emph{base case} on the ``smallest'' input value that has no recursion.
\end{enumerate}
A recursive function that does not satisfy both of these laws will run forever.
In python, these "forever loops" raise a \lstinline{RuntimeError},
but they are more generally called a \emph{stack overflow}.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
Recursion is closely related to \emph{mathematical induction}, which is covered in discrete math (MATH55) and several other courses.
At first, most students find recursion very difficult.
But eventually, writing recursive functions makes code easier to understand becuase it is much easier to write proofs about recursive functions than non-recursive functions.
In this class, we will focus on runtime proofs using recursion,
and the follow on graph algorithms course (CSCI148) covers more detailed proofs.
\end{note}
